## Container Dispatch
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

Container dispatch is one of the core features of `compose.mk`, and involves binding existing tasks (i.e. make-targets or scripts) to existing docker containers.

{{macros.img_link("escher-reptiles.jpg", mkdocs, width="70%", align='center', class=" ")}}

Targets might be custom (i.e. project-local), or might be part of the [compose.mk API]({{_}}/api), or might be orchestration that combines all of the above.  Containers might be existing images, or embedded images managed directly by `compose.mk`, or might come from embedded or external docker-compose specs.

Later in this page, we'll get into more details about [how/when container dispatch is useful](#but-why), and a [typical setup](#compose-file-boilerplate)  with `docker-compose.yml` that supports it.  For now though, let's start with what the idiom looks like and how it's used.

### Dispatch Basics
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

[Dispatch comes in several flavors](#other-kinds-of-dispatch), but we'll start with the support for docker-compose backed tool-containers. *(For more background, see the docs for [target-scaffolding]({{_}}/bridge) and using [`compose.import`]({{_}}/bridge/#other-import-statements)).

To illustrate the idea, consider the following project Makefile:

{{site.embed_twin('demos/container-dispatch.mk', 'demos/cmk/container-dispatch.cmk', github=github)}}

Stylistically there's a bunch of ways to write this, but you can probably tell this approach is python-inspired.  For one thing, the `__main__` target is a default entrypoint, and our "functions" support python style docstrings.  Plus, the blend of declarative and functional styles is similar to decorators[^decorator] when you clean it up:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/container-dispatch-min.mk', github=github)}}

This approach to dispatch uses target scaffolding in a way that's basically functional, but we'll get to something more declarative in the [next section](#namespace-style-dispatch).  And of course, this is still just a Makefile, so you could always write out target-bodies instead of using prerequisites:

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}
include compose.mk
$(call compose.import, file=demos/data/docker-compose.yml)
__main__:
	echo hello host `uname -n -v`
	${make} debian.dispatch/self.demo
	echo cleaning
self.demo:
	echo hello container `uname -n -v`
```

Running it looks like this:

{{macros.img_link("demo-dispatch.gif", mkdocs)}}

*What just happened?*  

You could say that what we have so far is a kind of notation where the following are roughly equivalent:

```bash {.cli_example}
# pithy invocation with compose.mk
$ ./demo

# the verbose alternative invocation
$ docker compose -f docker-compose.yml \
    run --entrypoint bash debian -c "make self.demo"
```

Nice, this is already **much** cleaner and more composable than the bash alternative, where you get lots of disconnected automation scripts that are littered with lots of `docker run ...` statements.  In fact, this is pretty similar to [`agent { docker { .. } }` in Jenkins Pipelines](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/docker/), or equivalent for github-actions, but it's much more portable and readable.

### Namespace-Style Dispatch
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The last section introduces container-dispatch in a functional paradigm, but for something that feels more declarative, you can use **namespace dispatch*.  This is especially helpful if you want to stay organized while building *lots* of targets with *lots* of containers across [maybe several compose-files]({{_}}/bridge/#other-import-statements). 

In such cases name-collisions might be inevitable, and you'll want fine-grained controls over namespacing to get something closer to an "absolute path" for the dispatch mechanics.  The next example is equivalent to the first, and begins to shed light on how the [`compose.import` macro arguments]({{_}}/bridge/#other-import-statements) can be used to create user-defined syntactic sugar[^sugar].

{{site.embed_twin('demos/container-dispatch-2.mk', 'demos/cmk/container-dispatch-2.cmk', github=github)}}

Similar to "private" targets that follow a leading-dot naming convention, using a blocky unicode symbol like `▰` isn't *actually* required, and of course you can call your namespace anything you want.  There's a few reasons for this weird looking style though and you might see various docs and demos using it.  Think of it at as a *naming convention that's approaching notation*.  The idea is that:

1. Sugar should be shortened, not longer than the syntax it's replacing
1. Sugar should be visually very easy to parse, and blocky unicode symbols qualify while also making it very clear this is a specific new kind of idiom.

If you decide to use it, this notation also has a property that it's *hard to use from the command line*, but it is *easy to use from file that already has several such instances*.  That's a feature!  To avoid confusion, targets intended *to run inside containers* probably should **not** run from the top-level.  And if you find yourself always watching to dispatch a private target using an invocation that routes a namespaced private target through the `▰/debian` container prefix, that's a good indication that it's actually **a project-level verb** and not just a component-action. Project-verbs are worthy of an explicit alias, because it's a separate category of automation and a separate API to curate.

### Toolboxes & Multiple Compose Files
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Putting it together, suppose your project involves so many tool containers that it is useful to separate them into different "tool boxes" by using different compose files.  By using `compose.import.as`, we can import to a namespace *without* cluttering the root.

{{site.embed_twin('demos/container-dispatch-3.mk', 'demos/cmk/container-dispatch-3.cmk', github=github)}}

This way of arranging things makes it clear that there are a few distinct toolboxes at play.  Of course, you can always call the private docs-generation target directly for testing if you *know* you have LateX tools available.  At the same time, the naming convention helps to serve as an important reminder that **your** LaTeX toolchain might be different than whatever coworkers or CI/CD is using.

### One-to-Many Dispatch
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Let's add another target to our project Makefile, demonstrating dispatching one target to multiple containers:

{{site.embed_twin('demos/double-dispatch.mk', 'demos/cmk/double-dispatch.cmk', github=github)}}

Again, the *`self`* prefix and the `▰/` namespace is just a convention, you can see discussion in the previous section.  The above looks pretty tidy though, and hopefully this helps to illustrate how the target / container / callback association works.  Running `demo.double.dispatch` looks like this:

{{macros.img_link("demo.double.dispatch.gif", mkdocs)}}

Meanwhile, the almost-equivalent-but-expanded pure bash version below is getting cluttered.

```bash {.cli_example}
# Clean, simple invocation with compose.mk
$ ./demos/double-dispatch.mk

# Verbose, fragile alternative
$ docker compose -f docker-compose.yml \
    run --entrypoint bash debian -c "make self.demo" \
  && docker compose -f docker-compose.yml \
    run --entrypoint bash alpine -c "make self.demo"
```

Eagle-eyed readers will note that even the verbose version above is *actually abbreviated*, because this doesn't mention [the volume-mount necessary for tool containers to get at project files]({{_}}/raw-docker/#tool-container-defaults)!

